  On the hardware + animation question

  Important technical reality first: multiple GPUs don't pool VRAM for a single 
  diffusion model — a video model runs on one card. So your best video card stays
  the 16 GB RTX 2000 Ada. The spares are still useful — as a parallel still-gen 
  worker or to run a smaller video model independently (the 3070 Ti is
  newer/bf16-capable but 8 GB; the 2080 Ti has more VRAM at 11 GB but older). The
  thing that actually unblocks generative video is the added storage (models are
  5–15 GB each; you only have 17 GB free now).

  There are two genuinely different animation routes, and they're good at
  different things:

  A · Procedural loop pipeline (no model, buildable right now): I add
  imageio-ffmpeg to the venv (tiny, no sudo) and write a small Python tool that
  animates the existing stills — chromatic split↔merge, probability-noise
  dissolve in/out, displacement-warp "fold churn," off-axis crossfades between
  two keyframes. This produces exactly the H3 effects you described, with
  frame-precise control, today, no hardware change. Limitation: it manipulates a
  still — the fold churns but doesn't invent new geometry, and it can't do the H4
  crowd scene.

  B · Generative video model (needs the storage add; runs on the 16 GB card):
  e.g. LTX-Video or Stable Video Diffusion — feed a FLUX keyframe, get true
  organic motion. Unlocks novel motion and eventually the H4 
  crowd-watching-an-organic shot. Limitation: less precise control over specific
  named effects, slower iteration, real setup.

  My recommendation: both, staged — let me build A now (it directly delivers your
  H3 loops and costs nothing), and in parallel you add storage so we can stand
  up B for organic motion + the crowd shot.



