  Design: polytope-overlay effect (true rotating 4D wireframe)
     
  A new effect that draws a genuine 4D polytope — rotated in 4D, projected
  4D→3D→2D — as a glowing wireframe composited over the crisp keyframe. New
  clips; nothing existing is removed.

  Polytope definitions — _polytope(shape) -> (verts Nx4, edges)

  - tesseract (8-cell): 16 verts = all (±1,±1,±1,±1); edges = vertex pairs at
  Hamming distance 1 → 32 edges.
  - 16-cell (cross-polytope): 8 verts = (±1,0,0,0) permutations; edges = all
  non-antipodal pairs → 24 edges.
  - 5-cell (4-simplex): 5 verts built deterministically by centering the R⁵ basis
  and projecting onto the sum-zero hyperplane via SVD (regular simplex, no
  hand-typed magic numbers); edges = all 10 pairs.

  Per-frame render

  1. Normalize verts to unit radius.
  2. 4D rotate by θ = 2π·t·turns through the xw/yw/zw planes (same rotation
  family as hyper_warp; integer turns ⇒ identity at t=0 and t=1 ⇒ seamless, exact
  loop).
  3. Project 4D→3D (perspective by w: d4/(d4−w)), then 3D→2D (perspective by z:
  d3/(d3−z)). d4=2.5, d3=3 keep denominators safe since |w|,|z|≤1.
  4. Map to pixels (center + size·half-image).
  5. Draw edges into an intensity buffer at 2× supersample (PIL ImageDraw.line,
  width width), downsample for anti-aliasing; make a Gaussian-blurred copy for
  glow and combine (glow*0.6 + sharp).
  6. overlay_rgb = intensity · color (cool blue-white default [0.6,0.8,1.0]);
  screen-blend over the keyframe: out = 1−(1−base)(1−overlay) — preserves the
  painting, adds luminous lines.

  No caching needed — it's ~10–32 lines per frame, cheap.

  Params: shape (tesseract|5-cell|16-cell), turns (int, 1), size (0.7), width
  (2), glow (6), color ([0.6,0.8,1.0]), d4 (2.5), d3 (3.0). 1 input.

  Wiring (new clips, all current kept)

  New gallery group "Animations · 4D polytopes" — 3 clips over crisp abstract
  bases:
  - anim_poly_tesseract.mp4 — abstract_fold_a, tesseract
  - anim_poly_16cell.mp4 — abstract_fold_b, 16-cell
  - anim_poly_5cell.mp4 — abstract_fold_a, 5-cell

  (11 existing → 14 total.)

  Testing

  _polytope vertex/edge counts (16/32, 8/24, 5/10) and valid edge indices; effect
  shape/dtype; loop-seamless (frame0≈frameN ≤1); overlay brightens (output >
  base sum); unknown shape → ValueError; render smoke. Plus the v1 loop-tolerance
  approach.

  Notes

  Reuses the gallery's existing <video> rendering (no gallery code change). Keeps
  hyper-warp as-is for the H3 character folds.

  ---
● Spec written and committed to
  docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-13-voidborn-polytope-overlay-design.md (on the
  feat/voidborn-animation-v2 branch). Please review it and let me know if you
  want any changes before I write the implementation plan.

  Worth a glance:
  - 5-cell coordinates — built deterministically via SVD of the centered R⁵ basis
  rather than hand-typed numbers, so it's a guaranteed-regular simplex.
  - Anti-aliasing — PIL's ImageDraw.line isn't AA, so I draw at 2× and downsample
  with LANCZOS. Costs a bit but the lines won't be jaggy.
  - Screen blend keeps the painting and only adds light (never darkens), so the
  crisp keyframe stays intact under the wireframe.
  - Color/size defaults are cool blue-white at 0.7–0.8 of the frame — all tunable
  per-clip in anims.json after first render.

